---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-restless-legs`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Restless Legs Syndrome',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Restless legs syndrome', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'G25.81', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Restless Legs Syndrome', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Restless Legs Syndrome: Iron First, Vitamin D Second"
  description="Restless legs syndrome and vitamin D — the iron/ferritin priority, small vitamin D supplementation evidence, and evidence-based RLS management."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-restless-legs", label: "Restless Legs" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Restless Legs Syndrome</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Restless legs syndrome (RLS) is neurologically related to central iron and
      dopamine dysfunction. Ferritin is the primary lab to check. Vitamin D
      deficiency associations exist but are secondary — correct deficiency, but
      don't skip the ferritin.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Iron first — the ferritin priority</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      RLS is a "brain iron deficiency" syndrome even at normal peripheral iron.
      Guidelines recommend checking ferritin and transferrin saturation; treating
      ferritin below 75 ng/mL with oral iron (ferric maltol or standard ferrous
      salts) even without frank anaemia. This is the highest-impact intervention
      for most RLS patients.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Vitamin D evidence</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Cross-sectional studies find lower 25(OH)D in RLS patients. Wali 2015
      randomised RLS patients to vitamin D or placebo and reported significant
      IRLS score improvement in the vitamin D group. Small trial. Broader
      evidence is limited.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical approach</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Any adult with RLS: check ferritin, transferrin saturation, CBC, TSH, 25(OH)D, magnesium, B12, glucose.</li>
      <li>Ferritin &lt; 75 ng/mL: iron supplementation (325 mg ferrous sulfate every other day, or ferric maltol).</li>
      <li>25(OH)D &lt; 30 ng/mL: correct with 2,000 IU/day cholecalciferol.</li>
      <li>Persistent moderate-severe RLS after nutrient repletion: neurology referral; dopamine agonists (pramipexole, ropinirole), alpha-2-delta ligands (gabapentin enacarbil, pregabalin).</li>
      <li>Avoid RLS triggers: caffeine after mid-day, alcohol, sedating antihistamines, some antidepressants.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> RLS with severe sleep disruption
      warrants prompt medical evaluation — the ferritin-and-iron pathway alone
      resolves symptoms in many patients.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-restless-legs" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
